To find the sum, we look at the first few powers of 2 modulo 7: \begin{align*}
2^0 &\equiv 1, \\
2^1 &\equiv 2, \\
2^2 &\equiv 4, \\
2^3 &\equiv 8 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}
\end{align*}Since $2^3 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}$, the powers of 2 modulo 7 repeat in cycles of 3.  Therefore, \begin{align*}
&1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + \dots + 2^{100} \\
&\quad\equiv 1 + 2 + 4 + 1 + 2 + 4 + \dots + 1 + 2 + 4 + 1 + 2 \\
&\quad\equiv (1 + 2 + 4) + (1 + 2 + 4) + \dots + (1 + 2 + 4) + 1 + 2 \\
&\quad\equiv \boxed{3} \pmod{7}.
\end{align*}

The prime factorization of $432 = 2^4 \cdot 3^3$. It follows that the sum of the divisors is equal to $(1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 3 + 3^2 + 3^3)$, as each factor of $432$ is represented when the product is expanded. We have to subtract $432$ so that we only count the proper divisors, so the answer is \begin{align*}
(1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 3 + 9 + 27) - 432 &= (31)(40) - 432\\
&= 1240 - 432\\
&= \boxed{808}.\\
\end{align*}

