[/asy]

By the triangle inequality on triangle $ABC$, \begin{align*}
x + 8 &> 10, \\
x + 10 &> 8, \\
8 + 10 &> x,
\end{align*} which tell us that $x > 2$, $x > -2$, and $x < 18$.

By the triangle inequality on triangle $CDA$, \begin{align*}
x + 12 &> 16, \\
x + 16 &> 12, \\
12 + 16 &> x,
\end{align*} which tell us that $x > 4$, $x > -4$, and $x < 28$.

Therefore, the possible values of $x$ are $5, 6, \dots, 17$, for a total of $17 - 5 + 1 = \boxed{13}$.

[asy]
pair D,EE,F,P,Q,G;

G = (0,0);
D = (1.2,0);
P= (-0.6,0);
EE = (0,1.6);
Q = (0,-0.8);
F = 2*Q - D;
draw(P--D--EE--F--D);
draw(EE--Q);
label("$D$",D,E);
label("$P$",P,NW);
label("$Q$",Q,SE);
label("$E$",EE,N);
label("$F$",F,SW);
draw(rightanglemark(Q,G,D,3.5));
label("$G$",G,SW);
[/asy]

Point $G$ is the centroid of $\triangle DEF$, so $DG:GP = EG:GQ = 2:1$.  Therefore, $DG = \frac23(DP) = 12$ and $QG = \frac13(EQ) =8$, so applying the Pythagorean Theorem to $\triangle QGD$ gives us $QD = \sqrt{QG^2 + GD^2} = \sqrt{64+144} = \sqrt{16(4+9)} = 4\sqrt{13}$, which means $DF = 2 QD = \boxed{8\sqrt{13}}$.

